WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:08.840
All right, friends, now we're going to cover three very easy quick functions in SQL to extract the

00:08.840 --> 00:09.760
parts of the dates.

00:09.760 --> 00:10.960
So they are very simple.

00:10.960 --> 00:14.640
The date function can return a day from a date.

00:14.640 --> 00:19.200
And in the same way, the month can return the month from a date.

00:19.200 --> 00:20.160
And guess what?

00:20.160 --> 00:23.400
The year can return a year from a date.

00:23.440 --> 00:29.600
Okay, so now in order to understand how they work, we have a date like this one 2025 August 20th.

00:29.640 --> 00:31.880
Sometimes you are not interested in the whole date.

00:31.880 --> 00:34.400
You would like to get only a part from this date.

00:34.520 --> 00:39.760
So you go and use the function day in order to extract the two digits.

00:39.880 --> 00:40.520
20.

00:40.560 --> 00:44.800
Now in other scenario, you might be interested in the month information, so you would like to get

00:44.800 --> 00:47.080
those two digits zero eight.

00:47.120 --> 00:53.320
So we can use the function month in order to extract the month information in order to get the August.

00:53.320 --> 00:58.880
So zero eight and one more situation where you want to have only the year information.

00:58.880 --> 01:05.200
So you are interested in the four digits 2025, so you can go and use the function here in order to

01:05.200 --> 01:06.040
extract it.

01:06.040 --> 01:09.120
So in the output, if you apply it you will get 2025.

01:09.120 --> 01:10.080
So it's very simple.

01:10.080 --> 01:12.360
This is how those three functions work.

01:12.400 --> 01:12.720
All right.

01:12.760 --> 01:14.720
Now let's check the syntax of those three functions.

01:14.720 --> 01:15.800
It's pretty easy.

01:15.800 --> 01:18.480
So we have it always like this a keyword called day.

01:18.520 --> 01:19.680
This is the function name.

01:19.680 --> 01:21.880
And then it accepts only one parameter.

01:22.040 --> 01:23.200
It is the date.

01:23.200 --> 01:24.720
The same things for the others.

01:24.720 --> 01:29.480
We have a function called month and it accepts as well only one parameter the date, and as well for

01:29.480 --> 01:30.600
the year the same thing.

01:30.600 --> 01:32.280
So the syntax is very straightforward.

01:32.280 --> 01:34.480
It accepts only one value the date.

01:34.480 --> 01:38.960
And we have the function name like the name of the bar that we want to extract.

01:39.000 --> 01:39.320
All right.

01:39.320 --> 01:43.760
So now let's try out those functions I will be working with the column creation time.

01:43.760 --> 01:49.040
So let's try for example extracting the year from the creation time using the year function.

01:49.040 --> 01:50.400
So it's going to be very simple.

01:50.440 --> 01:55.640
It's going to be year and then creation time like this.

01:55.640 --> 01:57.640
And let's call it year.

01:57.760 --> 01:58.280
That's it.

01:58.320 --> 01:59.530
Let's go and execute it.

01:59.570 --> 02:00.770
Now, as you can see, it's very simple.

02:00.770 --> 02:04.410
We have only one year 2025 from the creation time.

02:04.410 --> 02:09.130
So with that, as you can see we got a new column where we have only the year informations inside it.

02:09.130 --> 02:11.210
And this information come from the creation date.

02:11.210 --> 02:13.370
So we have only 2025.

02:13.410 --> 02:20.690
Now let's go and do the same for the month so we can have the same thing month creation time and let's

02:20.690 --> 02:21.530
call it month.

02:21.850 --> 02:23.370
So let's execute it now.

02:23.370 --> 02:26.770
As you can see in the output we got as well the number of the month.

02:26.770 --> 02:29.290
So we have here January, February and March.

02:29.290 --> 02:34.970
And those are information as well are extracted from the creation time and the same thing using the

02:35.010 --> 02:35.930
day function.

02:35.930 --> 02:37.610
So let's go and use that.

02:38.250 --> 02:42.250
So creation time and we call it day.

02:42.290 --> 02:47.490
So now as you can see the output we have the day part from the creation time.

02:47.490 --> 02:49.970
So here we have one 510 and so on.

02:49.970 --> 02:52.450
And all those informations come from the creation time.

02:52.450 --> 02:58.610
So as you can see those three functions are very simple and quick in order to extract parts from a date

02:58.610 --> 02:59.530
or date time.
